      "font" : "CMBX10",
      "font size" : 9.963,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Claim 15. We may assume ΛU[S] is 2-connected.",
      "kids" : [ {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 1,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 133.8, 476.224, 477.599, 534.752 ],
        "font" : "CMR10",
        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Proof. ΛU[S] must be connected, otherwise we could translate a connected component of ΛU[S] to form additional edges contradicting the maximality of ΛU[S]. Suppose ΛU[S] had a cut vertex v. When we remove v from ΛU[S] we create two connected components G1 = ΛU[S1] and G2 = ΛU[S2] with n1 and n2 vertices, and e1 and e2 edges."
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 2,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 133.799, 416.457, 477.517, 474.992 ],
        "font" : "CMR10",
        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Case 1: n1 < 4 or n2 < 4. Without loss of generality suppose n1 ≤ 3. If n1 = 1, there is 1 edge removed when deleting S1 from ΛU[S]. Applying the inductive hypothesis to the remaining graph we obtain e ≤ e(n − 1) + 1 ≤ e(n). So supposing that n1 ∈ {2,3}, then there are at most 6 edges removed when deleting G1 from ΛU. Then the inductive hypothesis implies"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 3,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 135.12, 388.236, 331.381, 405.272 ],
        "font" : "CMSY10",
        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "e ≤ 6(n − n1) − 4 6(n − n1) − 6 + 6 ≤ 6n −"
      }, {
